
<p>Steven Levy, writing at Wired with the scoop on the new MacBook Pros (<a href="https://apple.news/AaeR22fkURt6i_tk9bd_44g">News+ link</a>):</p>

<blockquote>
  <p>This turnaround is a big deal to Apple fans. Ternus shrugs off the
drama. “The Mac is first and foremost an indirect input device,
built around the trackpad and keyboard. But I think there’s times
when it’s comfortable and handy to have touch.”</p>

<p>Ternus and Apple want to make sure that the Mac faithful don’t
panic that the 42-year-old franchise is losing its identity to
Apple’s gangbusters mobile products. “We needed to get to a point
where adding touch didn’t make the product lesser in any way. It
didn’t make it heavier. It didn’t make it thicker. That came
together with the OLED screen, the best display we’ve ever put
into a Mac,” Ternus told me. “All of these things, and, oh yeah,
it has touch as well.”</p>
</blockquote>

<p>After a long fun day of hands-on exploration, briefings, and a slew of demos, not just most, but <em>all</em> of my concerns about touchscreen support adversely affecting the Mac UI have been allayed. I love it. These seem like exquisite machines even if you never want to touch the screen. I think what Ternus told Levy captures the truth of touch’s role: “All of these things, and, oh yeah, it has touch as well.”</p>

